              "accepted_answers": [
                [
                  "CAD"
                ],
                [
                  "computer-aided design"
                ]
              ],
              "hint": "C-A-D.",
              "explanation": "CAD stands for Computer-Aided Design."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "u1_l2",
          "title": "Types of views used on drawings",
          "theory": "Engineers use different **views** to visualize components from various angles:\n\n* **Plan**: A view from above (looking down).\n* **Elevation**: A view from the side (e.g., front elevation).\n* **Section (cross-section)**: A view inside an object, as if it were cut through.\n* **Exploded view**: A 3D view showing components spaced out (taken to pieces).\n* **3D views**: Often shown as **oblique projections** or **isometric projections**.\n\nNote: Two-dimensional and three-dimensional are often shortened to **2D** and **3D**.",
          "exercises": [
            {
              "type": "matching",
              "question": "Match the descriptions with the names of views used on drawings.",
              "pairs": [
                {
                  "term": "Elevation",
                  "definition": "A 2D view of the side of an object"
                },
                {
                  "term": "Section",
                  "definition": "A 2D view inside an object, as if it is cut through"
                },
                {
                  "term": "Plan",
                  "definition": "A 2D view, looking down on top of an object"
                },
                {
                  "term": "Exploded view",
                  "definition": "A 3D view, showing an assembly taken to pieces"
                },
                {
                  "term": "Oblique projection",
                  "definition": "A 3D view, with the 2D face of the object at the front"
                },
                {
                  "term": "Isometric projection",
                  "definition": "A 3D view, with a corner of the object at the front"
                }
              ],
              "hint": "Think about the angle and perspective.",
              "explanation": "Elevation is side view, Plan is top view, Section is internal view."
            },
            {
