          "id": "u12_l1",
          "title": "Carbon steels and alloy steels",
          "theory": "Steel is an alloy of iron (Fe) and carbon (C). It is divided into two main categories:\n\n* **Carbon steels**: Contain only iron and carbon. Grades include **mild steel** (low carbon), **medium carbon steel**, and **high carbon steel**.\n* **Alloy steels**: Contain iron, carbon, and alloying metals (e.g., chromium, nickel).\n    * **HSLA (High strength low alloy)**: Contains small amounts of alloying metals (less than 2%).\n    * **Stainless steel**: Contains chromium (Cr) and nickel (Ni); highly corrosion-resistant.\n    * **Tool steel**: Extremely hard; contains tungsten (W) or cobalt (Co). **High-speed steel** is used for drill bits.",
